<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canassatego</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1684–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Canasatego</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canassatego was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> nation who became a prominent diplomat and spokesman of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy in the 1740s. He first appears in the records at a treaty conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1742, and he was involved in several controversial land sales to British colonial officials. He is best known for a speech at the Treaty of Lancaster in 1744, in which he recommended that the British colonies form a confederacy as the Haudenosaunee had done. He was reportedly assassinated in 1750, perhaps by sympathizers or agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>.</p></div>
